    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/530054/parttime-lecturer-first-year-english": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - First Year English",
      "description": "Part-Time Lecturer - First Year English Apply now Job no: 530054 Work type: Faculty Part Time/Adjunct, Faculty Part Time - Talent Community Categories: Faculty Position Overview The Department of English and Communication at the University of Massachusetts Dartmouth seeks Part-Time Lecturers to teach in the First-Year English (FYE) program. Qualified candidates might start as soon as Fall 2026. Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: At the time of application, potential candidates must: hold a Master's degree or higher in Writing Studies, Rhetoric, English, or a related field, and have taught at least one semester of a comparable class at the college/university level Preferred Qualifications: Experience in teaching first-year writing, in developmental reading/writing, and/or dual enrollment First-Year writing courses. How to Express Interest Expressions of interest are reviewed on a rolling basis. Please submit a cover letter, CV, and the contact information of three references. About UMass Dartmouth UMass Dartmouth has transformed the lives of 40,000 alumni and distinguishes itself as a vibrant, public research university dedicated to engaged learning and innovative research. It is the only Massachusetts Tier 1 national research university south of Boston. Located on a beautiful 710-acre suburban campus on the South Coast of Massachusetts, UMass Dartmouth offers faculty the opportunity to work alongside talented students and colleagues in a community defined by genuine support and dedication. Advertised: 10 Jul 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/525446/parttime-lecturers-adult-nursing": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturers - Adult Nursing",
      "description": "Part-Time Lecturers - Adult Nursing Register Interest Job no: 525446 Work type: Faculty Part Time/Adjunct Categories: Faculty College of Nursing and Health Sciences Department of Adult Nursing Part-Time Lecturer Positions The Department of Adult Nursing invites applications for Part-time Lecturer (PTL) opportunities at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Opportunities are available for clinical and didactic assignments. Part-time faculty are expected to engage in course-based continuous quality improvement/assessment per college processes and be committed to rapid pedagogical and other academic process changes as driven by environmental conditions. Undergraduate Level Teaching: Opportunities primarily include clinical agency, clinical skills laboratory, and/or simulation instruction in the pre-licensure and accelerated BS Adult Health clinical courses. Didactic teaching opportunities are periodically available. Requirements: Preferred individuals for clinical education assignments will have a Master’s Degree in Nursing from an accredited program, recent clinical practice experience, and clinical education experience in higher education or a healthcare institution with a focus on Adult Health or Medical-Surgical Nursing. Individuals with a Bachelor’s degree in nursing must matriculate into a Master’s Program within 1 year or obtain NLN Clinical Nurse Educator certification within one year to be considered for hire per the Massachusetts regulation. Individuals for didactic assignments will have a graduate degree in Nursing from an accredited program and recent relevant practice experience with a focus on Adult Health or Medical-Surgical Nursing. Preferred individuals will have teaching experience in higher education or a healthcare institution. Graduate Level Teaching: Individuals will have the appropriate level graduate degree in nursing from an accredited program, as required for the teaching assignment. Individuals will have teaching experience in higher education or a healthcare institution and recent practice experience relevant to the teaching assignment. All individuals will have an active RN license in Massachusetts or eligibility for RN licensure in MA. Additional Information: One of five colleges at the university, The College of Nursing and Health Sciences has an enrollment of over 700 nursing students, including traditional, accelerated, and RN-BS undergraduates as well as graduate students in Master’s, DNP, and PhD programs. All programs are fully accredited by the CCNE through 2026. Instructions Please submit a letter of interest, current resume, and the contact information for three professional references. Individuals must be authorized for employment in the U.S. Employment-based visa sponsorship is not available. This campaign emphasizes ongoing opportunities and invites qualified individuals to express their interest in working as a part-time lecturer at the University of Massachusetts Dartmouth, rather than seeking applications for a specific position. Your information will be reviewed, and if your skillset and qualifications align, you will be contacted and invited to apply to positions as they become available. Advertised: 22 Oct 2025 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Register Interest Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/525277/parttime-lecturers-community-nursing": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturers - Community Nursing",
      "description": "Part-Time Lecturers - Community Nursing Register Interest Job no: 525277 Work type: Faculty Part Time/Adjunct Categories: Faculty College of Nursing and Health Sciences Department of Community Nursing Part-Time Lecturer Positions The Department of Community Nursing invites individuals interested in Part-time Lecturer (PTL) opportunities at the undergraduate and graduate level. Opportunities are available for clinical and didactic assignments. Part-time faculty are expected to engage in course-based continuous quality improvement/assessment per college processes and be committed to rapid pedagogical and other academic process changes as driven by environmental conditions. Undergraduate Level Teaching: Opportunities primarily include clinical agency, clinical skills laboratory, and/or simulation instruction in community, psychiatric mental-health, pediatric, and women’s and maternal child health pre-licensure and accelerated BS clinical courses. Didactic teaching opportunities are available in the RNBS track and possibly other tracks. Requirements : Preferred individuals for clinical education assignments will have a Master’s Degree in Nursing from an accredited program, recent clinical practice experience, and clinical education experience in higher education or a healthcare institution. Individuals with a Bachelor’s degree in nursing must matriculate into a Master’s Program within 1 year or obtain NLN Clinical Nurse Educator certification within one year to be considered for re-hire per the Massachusetts regulation. Individuals for didactic assignments will have a graduate degree in Nursing from a Nationally accredited program and recent relevant practice experience. Preferred individuals will have teaching experience in higher education or a healthcare institution. Graduate Level Teaching: Individuals will have the appropriate level of graduate degree in nursing from an accredited program or its equivalent, as required for the teaching assignment. Individuals will have teaching experience in higher education or a healthcare institution and recent practice experience relevant to the teaching assignment. All individuals will have an active RN license in Massachusetts or eligibility for RN licensure in MA. Additional Information: One of five colleges at the university, The College of Nursing and Health Sciences has an enrollment of over 700 nursing students, including traditional, accelerated, and RN-BS undergraduates as well as graduate students in Master’s, DNP, and PhD programs. All programs are fully accredited by the CCNE through 2026. Instructions Please submit a letter of interest, current resume, and the contact information for three professional references. Individuals must be authorized for employment in the U.S. Employment-based visa sponsorship is not available. This campaign emphasizes ongoing opportunities and invites qualified individuals to express their interest in working as a part-time lecturer at the University of Massachusetts Dartmouth, rather than seeking applications for a specific position. Your information will be reviewed, and if your skillset and qualifications align, you will be contacted and invited to apply to positions as they become available. Advertised: 22 Oct 2025 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Register Interest Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/530285/assistant-teaching-professor-of-communication-digital-media-production": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Communication: Digital Media Production",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Communication: Digital Media Production Apply now Job no: 530285 Work type: Faculty Full Time Categories: Faculty Assistant Teaching Professor of Communication: Digital Media Production Department of English & Communication The Department of English & Communication at UMass Dartmouth invites applications for an Assistant Teaching Professor starting September 1, 2027. The successful candidate will have a specialization in Digital Media Production. We seek an expert to teach in our B.A. in Communication and M.A. in Professional Writing & Communication programs. These programs are grounded in rhetorical studies and span the areas of technical communication, digital/multimodal communication, journalism, and strategic communication. Successful candidates will have a demonstrated record of undergraduate teaching and curricular development in these areas. Duties: The successful candidate will teach and advise undergraduate students in our BA Communication program, including but not limited to foundations courses in speech communication, communication ethics, and digital/media literacy, and may occasionally teach in our MA Professional Writing & Communication program. The successful candidate will also teach several courses per year in the University Studies program including Science Communication, Business Communication, and Technical Communication, in addition to other professional writing courses and communication courses. The candidate will also contribute to curricular development in teaching areas commensurate with expertise and program need. The standard teaching load for this position is four 3-credit courses per semester. The candidate will also contribute service to the department and university. Required qualifications: PhD in Communication or closely related field, with a specialization in Digital Media Production (such as podcasting, digital content creation and development). Degree must be conferred by September 1, 2027. Demonstrated experience teaching in Communication (or closely related field) at the university level. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated professional/practitioner experience in area including but not limited to: digital storytelling, generative AI, organizational, corporate, or nonprofit communications, or media industries. Demonstrated success teaching in area at the university level. The University of Massachusetts Dartmouth is one of five UMass campuses, located on the South Coast of Massachusetts between Cape Cod and Rhode Island in a region well known for its beautiful beaches and scenic harbors. About an hour from Boston and 45 minutes from Providence, the South Coast provides opportunities for rural or urban living with access to major metropolitan areas and natural coastal environments. We are an Affirmative Action, Equal Opportunity, Title IX Employer. To engage our increasingly diverse student population, the English & Communication department is committed to inclusive pedagogies. UMass Dartmouth supports professional development and service opportunities. SALARY: $70,000.00–$80,000.00 UMass Dartmouth offers exciting benefits such as: 75% Employer-Paid Health Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts Life Insurance Long Term Disability State Pension Retirement Plan Optional Retirement Savings Plans Tuition Credit (Employee, Spouse, & Dependents) Twelve (12) paid holidays And More! Benefits for Faculty Federation Application Materials: To apply, please submit a letter of interest outlining your experience, professional interests, and approach to teaching, a current CV, and the contact information for three professional references. Applicants must be authorized for permanent employment in the United States. Employment-based visa sponsorship is not available. Advertised: 05 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/530280/assistant-teaching-professor-health-and-society-healthcare-administration": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor - Health and Society & Healthcare Administration",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor - Health and Society & Healthcare Administration Apply now Job no: 530280 Work type: Faculty Full Time Categories: Faculty Assistant Teaching Professor – Health and Society & Healthcare Administration College of Arts and Sciences University of Massachusetts Dartmouth The Department of Economics in the College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Massachusetts Dartmouth invites applicants for an Assistant Teaching Professor in Healthcare Administration starting September 1, 2027. This position will also support the university’s Health and Society Program. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated record of teaching excellence and will teach courses in healthcare administration, public health, or a closely related field. This is a 9-month, non-tenure-track position with primary responsibilities for undergraduate instruction, undergraduate advising, and university service. Initial appointment is for two years, with possibility of renewal. The Department of Economics offers BA and BS degrees in Economics as well as a BS in Healthcare Administration The BS Healthcare Administration prepares students for careers in planning, directing, and coordinating medical and wellness services. This program is designed for healthcare professionals who want to advance their careers as managers or supervisors. The program is also well-suited to students who are looking to work in the healthcare industry with a focus on operations, administration, and management rather than the science-focused aspects of this profession. The interdisciplinary Health and Society Program (BA, BS) brings together social science, science, and humanities courses that explore the social, cultural, economic, political, and biological factors that impact human health and the ethical dimensions of health care practices and public health policies. One of the goals of the Health and Society program is to close equity gaps in allied health fields and support students’ pursuit of professions that impact population health. UMass Dartmouth is in the beautiful ocean side community of Dartmouth, about an hour south of Boston, half-hour east of Providence, and half-hour west of Cape Cod. It offers a world class education to undergraduate and graduate students each year in over 40 undergraduate and 23 graduate programs offered by the College of Engineering, College of Nursing, Charlton College of Business, College of Arts and Sciences, College of Visual and Performing Arts, School of Law and School for Marine Science and Technology. The College of Arts and Sciences, the Department of Economics, and the Health and Society Program value the ability to serve students from a broad range of cultural heritages, socioeconomic backgrounds, genders, abilities, and orientations. We seek a teaching faculty who values working in a collegial, collaborative environment, guided by a commitment to helping all students achieve their educational goals. The ideal candidate will embrace our values predicated on the primacy of student success and will support an inclusive, accessible, and equitable learning environment. Duties It is expected the successful candidate will be an engaging and effective teacher with a strong commitment to student learning and success. Responsibilities include teaching in-person courses in Healthcare Administration, Health and Society, and Economics. These courses may include Introduction to Health and Society, a capstone course in healthcare administration, and courses in social science research methods. The successful candidate will demonstrate the ability to implement inclusive and evidence-based teaching and learning approaches to ensure the academic success of a diverse population of students; collaborate with faculty to enhance curriculum; provide academic advising; and participate in university service activities. Minimum Qualifications: Candidates must have earned a doctoral degree in healthcare administration, public health, or a closely related field by September 1, 2027. Undergraduate teaching experience in healthcare administration, public health, economics, or a closely related field. Application Instructions: Applicants should submit: Letter of interest Curriculum Vitae Contact information for 3 references At a later date applicants may be asked to submit: Teaching Statement that details teaching philosophy Sample Syllabi SALARY: $70,000–$85,000 UMass Dartmouth offers exciting benefits such as: 75% Employer-Paid Health Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts Life Insurance Long Term Disability State Pension Retirement Plan Optional Retirement Savings Plans Tuition Credit (Employee, Spouse, & Dependents) Twelve (12) paid holidays And More! Benefits for Faculty Federation Applicants must be authorized for permanent employment in the United States. Employment-based visa sponsorship is not available. The review of applications will be ongoing until the position is filled. Advertised: 05 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/530279/assistant-professor-marine-biology-tenuretrack": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Marine Biology (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Marine Biology (Tenure-Track) Apply now Job no: 530279 Work type: Faculty Full Time Categories: Faculty Assistant Professor Position in Marine Biology The Department of Biology at the University of Massachusetts Dartmouth invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor beginning September 1, 2027. The Department is seeking candidates with a teaching and research focus in marine organismal biology. The successful candidate’s research approach will cross boundaries of traditional life sciences disciplines and addresses questions of broad significance. The research focus is open but should include locally relevant questions involving marine organisms (fishes, invertebrates, or marine mammals). Researchers whose programs include both fundamental and applied questions are encouraged to apply. The successful applicant will develop an externally funded research program and will be expected to teach specialized classes in their area of expertise as well as a foundational marine biology class focused on fishes, invertebrates, or marine mammals. The successful candidate’s research program will complement existing research strengths in the Department and have the potential to form new cross-disciplinary research collaborations. The Biology Department is an inclusive community with research strengths in marine biology, ecology, organismal and evolutionary biology, and genomics. The department benefits from well-established connections on campus with the School for Marine Science and Technology, the College of Engineering, and the Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry, as well as collaborations with other universities, research institutions, and agencies in the region. The University of Massachusetts Dartmouth is located in the beautiful oceanside community of Dartmouth, about an hour south of Boston, half-hour east of Providence, and a half-hour west of Cape Cod. It offers a world class education to undergraduate and graduate students each year in over 40 undergraduate and 23 graduate programs offered by the College of Engineering, College of Nursing, Charlton College of Business, College of Arts and Sciences, College of Visual and Performing Arts, School of Law and School for Marine Science and Technology. The College of Arts and Sciences and the Biology Department value the ability to serve students from a broad range of cultural heritages, socioeconomic backgrounds, genders, abilities, and orientations. We seek a faculty member who values working in a collegial, collaborative environment, guided by a commitment to helping all students achieve their educational goals. The ideal candidate will embrace our values predicated on the primacy of student success and will support an inclusive, accessible, and equitable learning environment. UMass Dartmouth has transformed the lives of 40,000 alumni and distinguishes itself as a vibrant, public research university dedicated to engaged learning and innovative research and serves as a catalyst for the region. Duties The successful candidate will demonstrate commitment to teaching excellence and mentorship of both undergraduate and graduate students in research. Teaching assignments for this position will include courses that are required in our B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. programs and commensurate with the successful candidate’s expertise. Development of a productive externally-funded research program is expected. Service to the department, college, and university is also expected. Minimum Qualifications An earned doctoral degree in biology or a closely related field, at least two years of postdoctoral research experience, and evidence of scholarly productivity such as peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations. Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated excellence in university-level teaching, a record of external funding or strong evidence of potential to attain external funding for research, and demonstrated success working with people from diverse backgrounds. Demonstrated commitment to fostering interdisciplinary thinking. Salary and startup funds are determined upon offer of employment. SALARY: $80,000–$95,000 UMass Dartmouth offers exciting benefits such as: 75% Employer-Paid Health Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts Life Insurance Long Term Disability State Pension Retirement Plan Optional Retirement Savings Plans Tuition Credit (Employee, Spouse, & Dependents) Twelve (12) paid holidays And More! Benefits for Faculty Federation To apply please send: A cover letter addressing interest in the position, previous research accomplishments, and commitment to teaching and mentorship of graduate and undergraduate students. A detailed statement of plans for developing and implementing an externally-funded research program (maximum two pages). A statement of teaching experience and philosophy (maximum two pages). Curriculum vitae. Complete contact information for three professional referees. Candidates must be authorized to work permanently in the U.S. on a full-time basis. Employment-based visa sponsorship is not available. Employment is contingent upon verification of background/credentials and authorization to work in the U.S. UMass Dartmouth is an Affirmative Action, Equal Opportunity, Title IX Employer. Advertised: 04 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/530292/assistant-professor-of-political-science-tenuretrack": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Political Science  (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Political Science (Tenure-Track) Apply now Job no: 530292 Work type: Faculty Full Time Categories: Faculty Assistant Professor Position in Political Science The Department of Political Science at the University of Massachusetts Dartmouth invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor starting September 1, 2027. The successful candidate will have primary expertise in American Politics, with a focus on public law, judicial politics, and/or law and legal studies. We also seek a candidate who demonstrates commitment to supporting the department's pre-law program through innovative teaching and student advising/mentorship. While the secondary concentration is open, candidates with a secondary focus on comparative politics or public administration are preferred. The College of Arts and Sciences and the Political Science Department value the ability to serve students from a broad range of cultural heritages, socioeconomic backgrounds, genders, abilities, and orientations. We seek a faculty member who values working in a collegial, collaborative environment, guided by a commitment to helping all students achieve their educational goals. The ideal candidate will embrace our values predicated on the primacy of student success and will support an inclusive, accessible, and equitable learning environment. The University of Massachusetts Dartmouth is in the beautiful ocean side community of Dartmouth, about an hour south of Boston, half-hour east of Providence, and half-hour west of Cape Cod. It offers a world class education to undergraduate and graduate students each year in over 40 undergraduate and 23 graduate programs offered by the College of Engineering, College of Nursing, Charlton College of Business, College of Arts and Sciences, College of Visual and Performing Arts, School of Law and School for Marine Science and Technology. UMass Dartmouth has transformed the lives of 40,000 alumni and distinguishes itself as a vibrant, public research university dedicated to engaged learning and innovative research. It is the only Massachusetts Tier 1 national research university south of Boston and serves as a catalyst for the region. Duties The successful candidate will maintain an active research agenda in public law, broadly framed, and will be an engaging and effective instructor with a strong commitment to student learning and success. Responsibilities include teaching undergraduate political science courses; maintaining an active research and publication agenda; implementing inclusive and evidence-based teaching and learning approaches to ensure the academic success of a diverse population of students; collaborating with faculty to enhance curriculum; providing academic advising; and participating in university service activities. Minimum Qualifications Candidates must have earned a PhD in Political Science, or a related field, by September 1, 2027; have demonstrated experience teaching at the collegiate level; have an active research and publication agenda; demonstrate a strong commitment to teaching excellence as well as diversity and inclusion. Preferred Qualifications Additional (secondary) specialization in comparative politics and/or public administration Demonstrated commitment to supporting the department's robust pre-law program through innovative teaching and student advising/mentorship. SALARY: $80,000.00–$90,000.00 UMass Dartmouth offers exciting benefits such as: 75% Employer-Paid Health Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts Life Insurance Long Term Disability State Pension Retirement Plan Optional Retirement Savings Plans Tuition Credit (Employee, Spouse, & Dependents) Twelve (12) paid holidays And More! Benefits for Faculty Federation Application Process Qualified applicants are invited to submit electronically a letter of interest that includes a statement on their research agenda, detailed curriculum vitae, concise statement (no more than 500 words) on teaching interests and experience, and contact information for at least three professional references. Employment is contingent upon verification of background/credentials and work authorization. Applicants must be authorized for permanent employment in the United States. Employment-based visa sponsorship is not available. Screening of complete applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applications submitted by November 30 will receive first consideration. UMass Dartmouth is an Affirmative Action, Equal Opportunity, Title IX Employer. Advertised: 04 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/530309/assistantassociate-professor-of-marine-biogeochemistry-or-fisheries-tenuretrack": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Marine Biogeochemistry or Fisheries (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Marine Biogeochemistry or Fisheries (Tenure-Track) Apply now Job no: 530309 Work type: Faculty Full Time Categories: Faculty POSITION ADVERTISEMENT Assistant/Associate Professor of Marine Biogeochemistry or Fisheries (Tenure-Track) The University of Massachusetts Dartmouth invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant or Associate Professor level in the Department of Fisheries Oceanography (DFO) or the Department of Estuarine and Ocean Sciences (DEOS) at the School for Marine Science and Technology (SMAST), beginning September 2027. SMAST is an internationally recognized center for interdisciplinary marine science and technology , addressing some of the most pressing challenges facing our oceans, including climate change, sustainable fisheries and aquaculture, coastal resilience, marine renewable energy, and ecosystem health. Our faculty develop innovative observations, technologies, and models to understand a rapidly changing ocean and translate that knowledge into solutions that benefit marine ecosystems, fisheries, communities, and industry. SMAST provides faculty with the opportunity to build independent research programs within a highly collaborative, interdisciplinary graduate school. We seek creative and innovative scholars with a strong record of research achievement commensurate with career stage and a commitment to teaching, mentoring, collaboration, and external engagement. Candidates are sought for either of the following areas: Marine Biogeochemistry. We seek a marine biogeochemist whose research addresses emerging challenges and opportunities relative to understanding, observing, and modeling open ocean and coastal systems. Areas of interest include nutrient and carbon cycling, stable isotope and tracer geochemistry, carbon sequestration and ocean alkalinity enhancement, ocean acidification, deoxygenation, greenhouse gas dynamics, and ecosystem resilience as well as marine pollution, including the fate and transformation of nitrogen, plastics and/or other contaminants in coastal environments. We welcome candidates whose research advances these areas through field observations, laboratory and experimental approaches, numerical modeling, data-driven and machine learning methods, or emerging observing technologies such as autonomous and sensor-based platforms. Marine Fisheries We seek a scientist with research interests in marine policy, governance, ecosystems, ocean modeling and AI, and fisheries technology, with a geographic focus on, but not limited to, the Northwest Atlantic Ocean. Emphasis is placed on interdisciplinary research, advanced technology and quantitative analysis to better understand and predict the impact of a rapidly changing ocean and human activity on marine fisheries and seafood supply and translating that knowledge into sustainable management and adaptative strategies. An exceptional setting for marine research. SMAST is located on the shore of Clark’s Cove in New Bedford, Massachusetts – the nation’s leading fishing port by value – and provides nearly 100,000 square feet of marine research and educational facilities. Resources include a marine research pier with extensive observing capabilities, more than 8,000 square feet of seawater laboratories, a 90,000-gallon acoustic-optic test tank, a marine biodegradability laboratory, and state-of-the-art conference and collaboration facilities. SMAST’s location provides direct access to coastal and offshore research environments and proximity to Boston, Providence, and Cape Cod. SMAST brings together more than 150 faculty, students, and staff working across marine science, technology, fisheries, policy, and resource management. Our faculty collaborates extensively with the fishing industry, government agencies, NGOs, and academic institutions, including the Massachusetts Division of Marine Fisheries. These partnerships provide exceptional opportunities for applied research, external funding, student training, and regional and national impact. UMass Dartmouth is a national research university serving more than 7,500 undergraduate and graduate students through nearly 60 undergraduate and 50 graduate programs. As the only Massachusetts national research university south of Boston, UMass Dartmouth is a catalyst for innovation and economic development across the South Coast and a growing center for marine science and technology. SMAST faculty are expected to develop independent, externally funded research programs; teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students; supervise graduate students and postdoctoral researchers; and contribute to their department, school, university, and discipline. Candidates must hold a PhD in a relevant field and demonstrate a strong record of scholarly achievement and a commitment to teaching, mentoring, and collaborative research. Minimum Qualifications: Education: Candidates must hold at the time of appointment an earned Ph.D. in Marine Science, Oceanography, or a closely related field. Experience: Candidates must have a record of teaching at the graduate (or undergraduate) level and research accomplishments through publication in peer-reviewed journals and leading or participation in externally funded research projects commensurate with years since PhD. Prior experience working with academic, government, and/or industry partners is also desirable. The level of appointment (Assistant or Associate Professor) will be determined at the time of hire based on the candidate's teaching experience as well as research accomplishments as compared with the tenure and promotion criteria in Scholarship and Professional Activities for SMAST. Other: Candidates must demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching, mentorship, and scholarly accomplishments, as well as a capability to build a strong research program with extramural support. Preferred Qualifications: A track record of successful grant proposal submission and funding as a principal investigator. A track record of graduate course development and student mentorship. Strong written and oral communication skills as demonstrated by a record of publications and presentations. A demonstrated ability to be a productive member of a research collaboration. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Required: Background and experience in a marine-related research area of strategic interest to SMAST. Application Process: Application packages should be submitted online and include the following documents: 1) a curriculum vitae; 2) a concise (1-2 page) statement on research plans; 3) a concise (1-2 page) statement on teaching philosophy and plans; and 4) a cover letter (5) the names and contact information for three referees who can be contacted for letters. Review of complete applications will begin November 09, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Employment is contingent upon verification of background/credentials and work authorization. Statement on diversity: SMAST deeply appreciates the value of a diverse, equitable, and inclusive community, and defines diversity broadly to include (among other things) diversity of genders, races and ethnicities, cultures, physical and learning differences, sexual orientations and identities, veteran status, and work and life experiences. UMass Dartmouth is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. To read about SMAST’s code of conduct and diversity statement, please see https://www.umassd.edu/media/umassdartmouth/smast/lab-pdf-files/SMAST-Code-of-Conduct-and-Diversity-Statement.pdf . To learn more about UMass Dartmouth’s commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please see https://www.umassd.edu/diversity/ . SALARY: Assistant Professor: $85,000.00–$105,000.00 Associate Professor: $105,000.00–$125,000.00 UMass Dartmouth offers exciting benefits such as: 75% Employer-Paid Health Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts Life Insurance Long Term Disability State Pension Retirement Plan Optional Retirement Savings Plans Tuition Credit (Employee, Spouse, & Dependents) Twelve (12) paid holidays And More! Benefits for Faculty Federation For further information about benefits for university faculty and Human Resources information and policies, please see https://www.umassd.edu/hr/ Advertised: 03 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/530306/assistantassociate-teaching-professor-music-athletic-bands-and-percussion": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Teaching Professor - Music - Athletic Bands and Percussion",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Teaching Professor - Music - Athletic Bands and Percussion Apply now Job no: 530306 Work type: Faculty Full Time Categories: Faculty Assistant/Associate Teaching Professor – Music: Athletic Bands and Percussion College of Visual and Performing Arts University of Massachusetts Dartmouth The College of Visual and Performing Arts at the University of Massachusetts Dartmouth invites applications for an Assistant/Associate Teaching Professor in Music: Athletic Bands and Percussion. This is a non-tenure-track nine-month renewable position beginning on September 1, 2027. The intention is that the successful candidate will also serve as Director of Athletic Bands. This assignment will carry separate compensation in addition to the nine-month academic base salary. Teaching responsibilities include world music and leading the percussion studio. Of special importance will be the continued development of a dynamic sports bands program. The teaching professor will work closely with music leadership as well as admissions, athletics, student government, and area schools. Finally, the Director of Athletic Bands component of this position requires duties outside of normal Monday-Friday work hours. Minimum qualifications: Master’s degree in music. Experience in sports bands, world music, and percussion. Excellent communication skills, the ability to work with students, administrators, teachers, and community partners. Preferred qualifications: Experience developing and leading sports bands. Knowledge of area high schools and recruiting approaches. Advanced training and experience in percussion and percussion methods. Application materials: Please submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, reference check authorization release form, and names and contact information for three professional references. The College of Visual and Performing Arts, with over 500 undergraduate and graduate students, is the flagship visual and performing arts college in the UMass system. CVPA offers six concentrations within Art, Design and Media Arts, along with degrees in Art Education, Art History, Interior Architecture and Design, and Music. UMass Dartmouth enrolls approximately 7,700 undergraduate and graduate students across more than one hundred academic programs. Centrally located between Boston, Providence, Cape Cod, and Newport, the university is dedicated to building a culturally diverse and inclusive faculty and staff committed to excellence in teaching, scholarship, and service. SALARY: Assistant Teaching Professor: $70,000–$80,000** Associate Teaching Professor: $75,000–$85,000** ** The successful candidate is also expected to serve as Director of Athletic Bands. This assignment will carry separate compensation in addition to the nine-month academic base salary. UMass Dartmouth offers exciting benefits such as: 75% Employer-Paid Health Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts Life Insurance Long Term Disability State Pension Retirement Plan Optional Retirement Savings Plans Tuition Credit (Employee, Spouse, & Dependents) Twelve (12) paid holidays And More! Benefits for Faculty Federation To apply, please submit a letter of interest, a current CV, and the contact information for three professional references. Applicants must be authorized for permanent employment in the United States. Employment-based visa sponsorship is not available. The screening of applications will begin on January 4, 2027, and will continue until the position is filled. Advertised: 03 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/530305/assistant-professor-of-civil-and-environmental-engineering-tenuretrack": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Civil and Environmental Engineering (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Civil and Environmental Engineering (Tenure-Track) Apply now Job no: 530305 Work type: Faculty Full Time Categories: Faculty UNIVERSITY OF MASSACHUSETTS DARTMOUTH Assistant Professor Position in Civil and Environmental Engineering The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEN) at the University of Massachusetts Dartmouth invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the assistant professor level beginning in September 2027. The Department is particularly interested in candidates with a teaching and research focus in the broad area of geotechnical engineering. Specific areas of interest include but are not limited to: offshore/coastal geotechnics, geotechnical design for sustainability and extreme events, soil/structure interaction, biogeotechnics, environmental geotechnics, energy geotechnics, synergies of data science, AI and geotechnical engineering, and geosystem resilience. The Department has existing expertise in reliability, structural optimization, recycled materials, marine turbulence, renewable energy, environmental remediation, environmental justice and related areas, with strong programs in data science, robotics and environmental/marine sensing across the College of Engineering. The University supports strong interdisciplinary collaborations between researchers in the College of Engineering, the College of Arts and Sciences, the School for Marine Science and Technology, and the Center for Scientific Computing and Data Science Research ( https://www.cscdr.umassd.edu/ ). The University of Massachusetts Dartmouth is located in the beautiful ocean side community of Dartmouth, about an hour south of Boston, half-hour east of Providence, and half-hour west of Cape Cod. It offers a world class education to more than 7,500 undergraduate and graduate students each year in over 100 undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the College of Engineering, College of Nursing, Charlton College of Business, College of Arts and Sciences, College of Visual and Performing Arts, School of Law and School for Marine Science and Technology. UMass Dartmouth has transformed the lives of 65,000 alumni and distinguishes itself as a vibrant, public research university dedicated to engaged learning and innovative research. It is the only Massachusetts Tier 1 national research university south of Boston and serves as a catalyst for the development of marine science and technology industries along the south coast, which is also home to the nation’s first offshore wind development. As such, the University is committed to the growth of a regional “Blue Economy.” While candidates that can contribute to this effort are encouraged to apply, the Department seeks applications across the full breadth of geotechnical engineering. The College of Engineering is comprised of six departments: Bioengineering, Civil and Environmental Engineering, Computer and Information Science, Electrical and Computer Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, and Physics. We offer 8 undergraduate degree programs, 8 concentrations, 11 graduate programs, and 8 accelerated BS-MS programs that serve more than 1,100 students. Our mission is to prepare graduates to meet 21st-century technological challenges and improve the well-being of society; create new knowledge in engineering, technology, and bioengineering; and engage and develop students to become leaders in their fields through service and scholarship. Duties The successful candidate is expected to develop a nationally recognized, externally funded research program; implement inclusive, evidence-based teaching and learning approaches to ensure the academic success of a diverse student population; advise undergraduate and graduate (MS & PhD) students; contribute to university and professional service; foster interdisciplinary collaborations; and actively engage in college and university diversity, equity, and inclusion initiatives. Minimum Qualifications Candidates must have earned bachelor’s and doctorate degrees in civil engineering or a closely related field at the time of appointment, be able to teach courses in geotechnical engineering, and demonstrate a strong commitment to teaching excellence and scholarly research as well as diversity, equity, and inclusion. Preferred Qualifications Strong candidates will have research experience in geotechnical engineering or a related engineering field and teaching experience in higher education. Postdoctoral research in an interdisciplinary setting is highly desirable. Candidates with a professional engineering license will be preferred. We especially encourage applications from scholars historically underrepresented in science, mathematics, and engineering, including veterans and individuals with disabilities. We seek candidates who have demonstrated experience in efforts to increase the recruitment and retention of women and members of historically minoritized groups in engineering. Application Process Qualified applicants are invited to submit a letter of interest, detailed curriculum vitae, concise statements on research plans, teaching philosophy, and diversity, and contact information for at least three professional references. Employment is contingent upon verification of background/credentials. Screening of complete applications will begin December 7, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. More information about the Civil and Environmental Engineering Department can be found at https://www.umassd.edu/engineering/cen/ SALARY: $105,000.00–$125,000.00 UMass Dartmouth offers exciting benefits such as: 75% Employer-Paid Health Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts Life Insurance Long Term Disability State Pension Retirement Plan Optional Retirement Savings Plans Tuition Credit (Employee, Spouse, & Dependents) Twelve (12) paid holidays And More! Benefits for Faculty Federation UMass Dartmouth is an Affirmative Action, Equal Opportunity, Title IX Employer. Advertised: 02 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/530304/assistant-professor-of-mechanical-engineering-tenuretrack-2-vacancies": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Mechanical Engineering (Tenure-Track) - 2 vacancies",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Mechanical Engineering (Tenure-Track) - 2 vacancies Apply now Job no: 530304 Work type: Faculty Full Time Categories: Faculty Assistant Professor (Tenure-Track) Mechanical Engineering Department The University of Massachu­­­setts Dartmouth invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level in the Department of Mechanical Engineering ( https://www.umassd.edu/engineering/mne/ ) beginning in September 2027. We are seeking outstanding candidates with research strengths in one or more of the following areas: smart manufacturing processes and systems design; AI/Machine Learning-driven manufacturing process optimization, monitoring, diagnosis, and prognostics; digital twins for manufacturing and engineered systems; advanced statistical modeling, data analytics, and decision-making under uncertainty; quality and reliability engineering; supply chain resilience and sustainable manufacturing systems; and human factors and human-autonomy collaboration in manufacturing. Candidates whose work bridges manufacturing, systems engineering, and industrial engineering are especially encouraged to apply. The University of Massachusetts Dartmouth (UMassD) is located in the beautiful ocean-side community of Dartmouth, about an hour south of Boston, a half-hour east of Providence, and a half-hour west of Cape Cod. It offers a world-class education to more than 7,500 undergraduate and graduate students each year in over 100 undergraduate and graduate (MS & PhD) programs offered by the College of Engineering, College of Nursing, Charlton College of Business, College of Arts and Sciences, College of Visual and Performing Arts, School of Law and School for Marine Science and Technology. UMassD has transformed the lives of more than 65,000 alumni and distinguishes itself as a vibrant, public research university dedicated to engaged learning and innovative research. UMassD is the only Massachusetts national research university south of Boston and serves as a catalyst for the development of marine science and technology industries along the south coast of Massachusetts. The Department of Mechanical Engineering combines a tradition of excellence in undergraduate education with a strong research culture. The successful candidate will join a world-class research team with existing strengths in a wide array of interdisciplinary fields including fracture and mechanical behavior of composites, damage sensing and diagnostics, additive manufacturing, multiphase heat and mass transfer modeling, experimental fluid mechanics with fluid structure interaction, boundary layer visualization and 3D imaging, turbulence and multiphase flows, super-hydrophobic surfaces, scientific computing, renewable energy with emphasis on the blue economy, energy storage materials, medical robotics, and micro-scale sensing/actuation. All faculty members are active in externally funded research grants and contracts, with funding from a variety of sources, most significantly NSF, ONR, NIH and DOE. We particularly welcome candidates whose research complements existing departmental and university strengths in marine and blue-economy technologies, robotics and autonomous systems, advanced materials, energy, and fluid/thermal sciences. Duties: The successful candidate will be expected to develop a nationally recognized, externally funded research program; teach and advise undergraduate and graduate (MS & PhD) students; contribute to university and professional service; foster interdisciplinary collaborations; and engage in college and university diversity, equity and inclusion initiatives. Minimum Qualifications: Candidates must have earned bachelor’s and doctorate degrees in Mechanical engineering or a closely related field at the time of appointment and demonstrate a strong commitment to teaching excellence and scholarly research as well as diversity, equity, and inclusion. Preferred Qualifications: Strong candidates will have research experience in a mechanical engineering field and teaching experience in higher education. Postdoctoral research in an interdisciplinary setting is highly desirable. We are especially interested in receiving qualified applications from underrepresented minorities and women. Candidates should demonstrate strong research skills and a promising or established record in one or more of the following areas. (a) Intelligent Manufacturing & Digital Engineering: Research on advanced and additive manufacturing, cyber-physical systems, digital twins, AI-enabled process monitoring and control, data-driven modeling, optimization, quality, reliability, and decision-making under uncertainty; and (b) Resilient, Sustainable & Human-Centered Systems: Research on resilient and sustainable manufacturing and supply chains, circular economy systems, systems-of-systems design and engineering, human-autonomy collaboration, human factors, and healthcare systems engineering. Candidates with research interests that align with nationally strategic manufacturing sectors, including semiconductors, electric vehicles, rechargeable batteries, critical minerals recovery, marine and ocean technologies, clean energy, robotics and autonomous systems, energy storage, and other emerging manufacturing sectors. are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate courses in advanced manufacturing concentration and graduate courses in the Industrial and Systems Engineering program, including but not limited to design for manufacturing, advanced manufacturing processes, operations research, probability and statistics, quality engineering, human factors, simulation, and systems engineering. Collaboration with interdisciplinary teams across the college and university is highly valued, and candidates with the potential to contribute to multi-investigator center-scale proposals are preferred. SALARY: $105,000.00–$125,000.00 UMass Dartmouth offers exciting benefits such as: 75% Employer-Paid Health Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts Life Insurance Long-Term Disability State Pension Retirement Plan Optional Retirement Savings Plans Tuition Credit (Employee, Spouse, & Dependents) Twelve (12) paid holidays And More! Benefits for Faculty Federation Application Process: Qualified applicants are invited to submit a letter of interest, detailed curriculum vitae, concise statements on research plans, teaching philosophy, and diversity, and contact information for at least three professional references. Employment is contingent upon verification of background/credentials. Screening of complete applications will begin on December 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Advertised: 02 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.umassd.edu/en-us/job/530428/assistant-teaching-professor-economicshealth-spring-20271-semester": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor - Economics/Health Spring 2027(1 semester)",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor - Economics/Health Spring 2027(1 semester) Apply now Job no: 530428 Work type: Faculty Full Time Categories: Faculty The College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Massachusetts Dartmouth invites applications for a one-semester Assistant Teaching Professor position to teach four in-person sections of courses serving the interdisciplinary Health and Society program and the Healthcare Administration program. We seek a dynamic and effective teacher with a degree (MPH, MA, PhD, or ABD) in public health, health economics, or a closely related field. The qualified candidate should be able to teach in person, either Intro to Health and Society, Health Economics or a topics course in your area of expertise. To be considered for this position, please apply and include your CV, cover letter addressed to Dr. Kristen Abatsis McHenry and Dr. Neal Olitsky. We are looking to begin zoom interviews immediately. The position is expected to start no later than January 25, 2027. Responsibilities include teaching four in person courses in the Spring semester. More information can be found at Health and Society-College of Arts and Sciences and Health Care Administration-College of Arts and Sciences at University of Massachusetts Dartmouth. University of Massachusetts Dartmouth is located on the South Coast of Massachusetts, Dartmouth near some of the most beautiful coastlines in the United States. Dartmouth is also conveniently located near Cape Cod, Providence, Newport, and Boston. New York is a three-hour train ride away. The College of Arts and Sciences at University of Massachusetts Dartmouth values the ability to serve students from a broad range of cultural heritages, socioeconomic backgrounds, genders, abilities, and orientations. The College of Arts and Sciences is committed to recruiting outstanding candidates who reflect the diverse identities of our student body. Minimum Qualifications: Candidates must have (MPH, MA, PhD, or ABD) in public health, health economics, or a closely related field. Undergraduate Teaching experience in a related field SALARY: $35,000–$42,500 UMass Dartmouth offers exciting benefits such as: 75% Employer-Paid Health Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts Life Insurance Long Term Disability State Pension Retirement Plan Optional Retirement Savings Plans Tuition Credit (Employee, Spouse, & Dependents) Twelve (12) paid holidays And More! Benefits for Faculty Federation To apply, please submit a letter of interest, a current CV, and the contact information for three professional references. Applicants must be authorized for permanent employment in the United States. Employment-based visa sponsorship is not available. Advertised: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    }
